    DESIGN DECISIONS LOCKED IN BY THIS FILE
    =======================================
      - Aggregation discipline (AAP Section 0.5.1, this report's row):
        type = bar. Per agent-prompt Mandatory Field Specification,
        chart-type reports populate <type>, <field>, and <aggregate>;
        they leave <field_list/> and <aggregation_source/> empty. The
        rendered output is one vertical-bar chart with one bar per
        distinct value of `status`.

      - Group-by discipline (agent-prompt Rule 4 verbatim):
        <field>status</field>. The Choice field defined in
        ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_status.xml. The six bars correspond
        to the six Choice values defined in
        ../choices/sys_choice_case_status.xml: Draft, Open, In Progress,
        Pending, Resolved, Closed (Title Case, "In Progress" with single
        ASCII space - NOT "InProgress", NOT "in_progress", NOT
        "IN PROGRESS").

      - COLUMN NAME OF THE GROUP-BY (measured, not assumed): the
        group-by dimension is carried in the `field` column, whose
        label is "Field Name". `sys_report` has NO `group_by` column
        on this release - sys_dictionary for sys_report offers
        `field`, `additional_groupby`, `group` (a reference to
        sys_user_group), `sc_groupby_item_id` and
        `sc_groupby_variable_id`, and nothing named `group_by`. An
        earlier revision of this file emitted <group_by>status</group_by>;
        the loader silently discarded the unknown element, `field`
        stayed empty, and the Report renderer then fell back to the
        alphabetically-first field on the table (Assigned Agent), so
        the chart drew a plausible-looking but semantically WRONG
        dimension with no error and no empty state. The element is
        now <field>status</field>, matching the sibling
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml, which always
        rendered correctly precisely because it named its column in
        `field`. The `group_by` element has been removed outright
        rather than left empty: it is not a column, so emitting it -
        even empty - only invites the next reader to believe it is one.
        The same applies to the `format` element the earlier revision
        emitted; sys_report has no `format` column either, and both are
        now gone from all eight report artifacts.

      - Filter discipline (agent-prompt Rule 5 verbatim):
        <filter/> empty. This report counts ALL cases regardless of
        status, priority, or assignment. The Manager View dashboard is
        intentionally an unfiltered aggregate-perspective view -
        per-viewer filtering belongs to Agent Workspace's "My Open
        Cases" list (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml) and
        "My Overdue Tasks" list (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_overdue_tasks.xml).
        Note: x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml DOES filter out Resolved/Closed
        because it serves an operational queue purpose, but THIS report
        retains Closed cases because the Manager View needs visibility
        into terminal-state volume to assess case throughput.

      - Aggregation discipline (agent-prompt Rule 6 verbatim):
        <aggregate>COUNT</aggregate>. Counts records per status bucket.
        <aggregation_source/> empty (COUNT does not require a numeric
        source field; it counts rows, not values).

      - Field-list discipline (agent-prompt Rule 9 verbatim):
        <field_list/> empty. Chart reports do NOT specify a field list;
        the field list is meaningful only for `type=list` reports per
        the sibling pattern in ../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml
        (which uses <field_list>number,subject,priority,status,opened_date</field_list>).
        For chart reports, the chart-type and group-by fully determine
        what is rendered.

      - Per-viewer semantics: this report does NOT reference any
        user-specific identity (e.g., gs.getUserID()); it returns the
        SAME aggregated counts for every viewer. Therefore:
          1) The platform does not need to re-compute the aggregate
             per-user, only per-cache-cycle. <is_real_time>false</is_real_time>
             below permits cached evaluation.
          2) Different roles see the same numbers (modulo what their
             ACL permits them to read). For a `case_viewer`, all six
             bars appear (read-all ACL). For a `case_agent`, only
             bars for cases assigned to them or their group appear
             (assigned-only ACL per AAP Section 0.5.6). For a
             `case_manager`, all six bars appear (full ACL). The Manager
             View dashboard is gated to `case_manager` per AAP Section
             0.4.4, so the typical viewer sees the full team picture.

      - Why "type=bar" not "type=pie": per AAP Section 0.4.4 the Manager
        View dashboard pairs different chart types to different
        cardinalities of categorical data:
          - Status (6 buckets): bar chart - magnitude comparison
            across many discrete buckets favors absolute-count
            readability. The eye reads vertical bars left-to-right
            against a common baseline more accurately than radial
            slices when N >= 5 buckets. The same data is ALSO rendered
            on the Agent Workspace dashboard as a donut/pie via
            ../reports/x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml because at-a-
            glance proportional readability is more important for an
            individual contributor's morning-coffee dashboard than
            absolute-count comparison.
          - Priority (4 buckets): bar chart - same rationale at lower
            cardinality; ordinal urgency tiers benefit from left-to-
            right linear ordering.
          - Type (2 buckets, extensible): donut/pie chart -
            proportion comparison across a small number of buckets
            favors "what percentage is each?" readability.
        The deliberate diversity of chart types is a UX-quality
        decision per AAP Section 0.4.4 and is NOT to be flattened
        into "all bar" or "all pie" for consistency.

      - Why this report co-exists with x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml:
        per AAP Section 0.4.4 widget inventory, the SAME aggregation
        (group by status, COUNT) appears on BOTH dashboards but with
        DIFFERENT chart types and DIFFERENT audiences:
          * Agent Workspace -> x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml
            (type=pie, donut rendering) - quick visual proportion
            check for the agent's morning standup.
          * Manager View -> THIS file x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status.xml
            (type=bar) - precise count comparison for the manager's
            operational review.
        The two reports are deliberately separate sys_report records,
        each bound to its respective dashboard widget by `name`. They
        share the underlying GlideAggregate query shape but render
        independently.

      - Cache discipline: <is_real_time>false</is_real_time>. The
        platform may cache the aggregated counts and refresh on a
        cycle (typically 5 minutes for the Reports module). For a
        POC dashboard with 10 demo cases this distinction is
        cosmetic; for a production case-management deployment with
        thousands of cases it materially affects dashboard load
        time. The default-off setting matches the sibling reports
        (../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_type.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml).

      - Chart-size discipline: <chart_size>medium</chart_size>. The
        platform-default rendering size for embedded dashboard widgets.
        Matches the sibling reports.

      - Scope binding: <sys_scope>x_casemgmt</sys_scope> and
        <sys_package source="x_casemgmt">x_casemgmt</sys_package>
        bind THIS record to the same scoped-app row that the parent
        sys_app record (../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml)
        publishes via the sys_scope record at
        ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (where <name>x_casemgmt</name>
        and <source>x_casemgmt</source>). Cross-file consistency: the
        sibling reports (x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_type.xml,
        x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml, x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml,
        x_casemgmt_my_overdue_tasks.xml, x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml,
        x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml) use identical scope-reference
        values, ensuring all reports resolve to the same sys_app row at
        preview time. Reference-by-name not reference-by-sys_id per AAP
        Section 0.7.2 (no hardcoded sys_ids constraint).

      - <sys_id> determinism: the value
        deeaca81d8626c9c2cc2974716efd08c is the lowercase MD5 of the
        canonical record name "x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status". This
        matches the convention documented in
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_type.xml lines 232-253
        (md5("x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_type") = f35f0a22b19d6e5385538eb3febb1a84),
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status.xml lines 223-242
        (md5("x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status") = 32ae52ab8735ea76587edee6130abc06),
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml lines 219-236
        (md5("x_casemgmt_my_open_cases") = ee04fa982b136ad35c6fe1512826f6ab),
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml lines 233-247
        (md5("x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close") = 79fabb5f5754b629205b2382face3ebb),
        and ../reports/x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml lines 71-84
        (md5("x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d") = 95b2127b026a92ba5e041a8d4d7277ec).
        Deterministic derivation makes the Update Set re-importable
        on a fresh PDI without sys_id collisions: re-importing the
        same XML on the same or any future PDI resolves the same
        sys_id and idempotently updates the same report row rather
        than creating a duplicate. This stability is REQUIRED because
        the Manager View dashboard
        (../dashboards/pa_dashboards_x_casemgmt_manager_view.xml)
        references THIS report by `<name>x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status</name>`,
        and the dashboard widget binding must resolve consistently
        across re-imports.

      - Sharing discipline (AAP Section 0.4.4 "agents and managers see
        operationally relevant aggregate views on day one", and AAP
        Section 0.7.3 Gate 6 "accessible to users with correct
        roles"): <roles>x_casemgmt_case_manager,x_casemgmt_case_agent,
        x_casemgmt_case_viewer</roles>. `sys_report.roles` is a
        user_roles column holding role NAMES (no sys_ids, per AAP
        Section 0.7.2), and it is what makes a report reachable by a
        user who is neither its owner nor an admin. With it empty, a
        report is private: an impersonated x_casemgmt_demo_agent
        opening this report received a blank page reading "Report
        visible only to a specific user or group" even though the
        report's filter was correct. All three scoped roles are
        listed because all three personas are legitimate readers of
        aggregate case data - the row-level perimeter is enforced by
        the table ACLs (AAP Section 0.5.6), not by report sharing, so
        an agent still only sees the rows their ACLs permit.
        <user>GLOBAL</user> is the other half of the fix, and the
        load-bearing half: the platform's own read ACL on sys_report is
        a script, and it consults `roles` ONLY on its `isGlobal` branch -
        `isGlobal` is `current.user.toString() == "GLOBAL"`. With `user`
        empty the report is private to whoever created it, the branch
        that reads `roles` never runs, and an impersonated
        x_casemgmt_demo_agent got a blank page reading "Report visible
        only to a specific user or group" even with all three roles
        listed. `sys_report.user` is a 40-character STRING (not a
        reference) whose magic values are 'GLOBAL' (visible to everyone,
        narrowed by `roles`) and 'group' (shared through
        sys_report_users_groups); 265 of 300 sampled out-of-box reports
        carry 'GLOBAL' with `roles` as the restriction, which is exactly
        the shape this report now ships. 'GLOBAL' is a literal platform
        keyword, not a sys_id, so the AAP Section 0.7.2 no-hardcoded-
        sys_id constraint still holds.

